class:: FeatureSmooth
summary:: One-pole smoothing at feature update instants
categories:: UGens>Analysis
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/Lag

description::
Updates y = exp(-frameTime/time)*previous + (1-exp(-frameTime/time))*input at accepted update instants. The first accepted value initializes the state directly. time is the 1/e settling constant, unlike the 60 dB lag-time convention used by vanilla Lag.

A negative trigger accepts every control block. A nonnegative trigger accepts only positive edges and holds the output between edges; this lets the caller align updates with analysis frames. frameTime must match that regular update spacing. Irregular triggers do not create a nonuniform regression. Nonfinite input is replaced by zero. Cost and state are constant. This is an independent one-pole low-pass recurrence. Vanilla Lag has a related purpose, while this UGen can update only when a feature frame arrives and uses a 1/e time constant.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: in
Control-rate or scalar feature, with multichannel expansion for feature arrays.

argument:: time
Nonnegative 1/e smoothing time in seconds; zero bypasses smoothing.

argument:: trigger
Negative: update every control block. Otherwise update on positive edges.

argument:: frameTime
Seconds between accepted values. Zero uses the server control period; supply the actual period when triggering sparsely.

returns::
Smoothed input value.

examples::
A level-derived feature updates 25 times per second. Its processed dynamics control the filter and reference tone, making the utility audible.
code::
(
Routine({
    ~mir2Buffer = Buffer.read(s, ExampleFiles.child);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);
        var tick = Impulse.kr(25);
        var feature = Latch.kr(Amplitude.kr(source, 0.005, 0.02).linlin(0, 0.3, 200, 3000), tick);
        var value = FeatureSmooth.kr(feature, trigger: tick, frameTime: 0.04);
        var control = value.linlin(200, 3000, 0, 1).clip(0, 1);
        var filtered = RLPF.ar(source, control.linexp(0, 1, 250, 5000), 0.25);
        (filtered * 0.25 + SinOsc.ar(control.linexp(0, 1, 160, 700), 0, 0.025))
            * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.5) ! 2
    }.play;
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
Stop and release the buffer:
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
~mir2Buffer.free;
)
::

